Doron Zeilberger is a renowned Israeli-American mathematician celebrated for his contributions to combinatorics, hypergeometric summation, and ultrafinitism, and for pioneering the use of computers in mathematical proofs and experimental mathematics

Biography and Education
Doron Zeilberger was born on July 2, 1950, in Haifa, Israel. He obtained his B.Sc. in mathematics from the University of London in 1972 and completed his Ph.D. at the Weizmann Institute of Science in 1976, under the supervision of Harry Dym, with a thesis titled "New Approaches and Results in the Theory of Discrete Analytic Functions"
He is currently a Board of Governors Professor of Mathematics at Rutgers University

Contributions to Mathematics
Zeilberger has made substantial contributions to several areas of mathematics, particularly combinatorics, q-series, and hypergeometric identities. He is known for:
Wilf–Zeilberger Theory (WZ Theory): Developed with Herbert Wilf, this framework allows automated proofs of hypergeometric identities, fundamentally changing computer-assisted combinatorics 
Alternating Sign Matrix Conjecture: Zeilberger provided the first proof, involving collaboration with nearly 100 volunteer checkers and extensive use of computational verification 
q-TSPP Conjecture: Proved in 2011 alongside Manuel Kauers and Christoph Koutschan for a problem posed by Andrews and Robbins 
Contributions to experimental mathematics, emphasizing that mathematics can be guided and even performed by computers 
He is known for crediting his computer, Shalosh B. Ekhad, as a co-author on papers, reinforcing his belief in machine-assisted proofs 

Mathematical Philosophy: Ultrafinitism
Zeilberger is a vocal ultrafinitist, a philosophical stance rejecting the conventional infinite in mathematics. He argues that:
Mathematical objects should be constructible and finite, reflecting real-world physical limits 
Traditional concepts of infinity are practically unverifiable and therefore of limited value.
Computers, with their finite capacity, enable rigorous proofs that reflect this philosophy 
This perspective challenges mainstream mathematics and emphasizes explicit, computationally feasible constructions over classic proofs relying on infinite sets or abstract arguments 

Awards and Recognition
Zeilberger has received numerous accolades:
Lester R. Ford Award (1990)
Leroy P. Steele Prize for Seminal Contributions to Research (1998) for developing WZ theory 
Euler Medal (2004), recognizing his innovative use of algorithms in combinatorics
David P. Robbins Prize (2016) for work on q-TSPP conjecture
Member of the inaugural class of American Mathematical Society Fellows (2013)

Selected Interests and Legacy
Zeilberger is engaged in teaching computers to perform mathematics, believing that in the future computers will surpass humans in proving and discovery
His favorite mathematical objects include alternating sign matrices, and he frequently focuses on problems amenable to algorithmic or computational approaches
Beyond mathematics, he enjoys chess problem-solving, particularly two-move puzzles, though humorously noting he is not a prodigious player
Overall, Doron Zeilberger stands out as a mathematician who bridges classical combinatorics with computational methods, challenges conventional views on infinity, and envisions a future where mathematics is an interactive endeavor between humans and machines
 
